WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.840
There are two moments in “Planet of Lana” that&nbsp;
made me realize I was playing something special.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:14.280
The first was, literally, a big moment. The&nbsp;
camera pulled back and an unfathomably large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:19.980
creature swam under my boat. Its body seemed&nbsp;
to keep going and going and there was a sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.980 --> 00:00:26.700
I’d surely be capsized and eaten — but instead it&nbsp;
simply passed on, leaving me with an overwhelming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:33.120
feeling that I wasn’t the center of this world.&nbsp;
The second moment was small: I misjudged a jump,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:40.260
and a predator caught me. Okay, time to try again.&nbsp;
After respawning, I decided to outrun the enemy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:46.200
since that’s… usually something you can do&nbsp;
in video games. I didn’t even get close.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:51.540
It's rare that a game makes you feel&nbsp;
truly unimportant — like you’re just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:56.880
tiny part of a much larger environment. When&nbsp;
a title successfully instills this feeling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:02.400
it can be like witnessing a sort of magic&nbsp;
trick. So what I’d like to do today is take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:08.340
you backstage and explore how different games&nbsp;
succeed in making you part of their ecosystems…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:16.020
Okay, so how do you get past a predator in&nbsp;
“Planet of Lana?” The answer is you have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:21.480
move slowly — learning the route of an enemy —&nbsp;
be they biological or mechanical — and finding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:27.780
a way to evade them altogether. From very early&nbsp;
on, the title establishes that you’re much too&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:33.600
insignificant to overcome dangers directly.&nbsp;
Instead, “Planet of Lana” is a game all about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.700
understanding the ecosystem: knowing how different&nbsp;
threats operate, and putting that knowledge into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.700 --> 00:01:44.520
practice to escape creatures much higher up the&nbsp;
food chain than you are. The character you play as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:51.240
is not particularly skillful. Your fastest running&nbsp;
speed is a leisurely jog. Your jump is just barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.940
enough to cross most gaps. You fall and stumble&nbsp;
down cliffs most video game characters wouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.940 --> 00:02:04.020
even be remotely bothered by. Unfortunately, the&nbsp;
land itself feels hostile. Eyes peer out from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:09.780
the darkness of most environments. And with no&nbsp;
health-bar, you’re always one wrong move away from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:15.960
game over. But if you pay attention, you realize&nbsp;
the game is constantly teaching you about how its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:21.240
ecosystem works. Early in “Planet of Lana,”&nbsp;
there’s a part where a tiny creature scuttles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.240 --> 00:02:27.540
under a rock as you wander by. Later, you pass a&nbsp;
giant rock, and it lumbers after you on hundreds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:32.880
of tiny feet. The game is full of these little&nbsp;
moments of internal consistency. And once you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:39.240
learn the rules of how the environment functions,&nbsp;
the path forward becomes clear. [web sounds]&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:46.020
But what about a game where you&nbsp;
experience an ecosystem not as a human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:52.320
but something a bit smaller. Introducing:&nbsp;
“Webbed.” A physics-based platformer where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:57.840
you play as a peacock spider. Moving around in&nbsp;
“Webbed” is quite difficult. At least for me,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:03.960
I found my first attempts at traversal wildly&nbsp;
messy. Instead of gracefully swinging on webs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:10.440
my spider careened around at high speeds, crashing&nbsp;
repeatedly into the foliage. My initial attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.440 --> 00:03:16.620
at spinning a fly-catching web was equally sad —&nbsp;
yielding a crummy, asymmetrical disaster that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:22.380
utterly ineffective. And this was a real problem,&nbsp;
because in the virtual ecosystem of “Webbed,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.380 --> 00:03:28.260
nearly every insect is larger than you are. And&nbsp;
the ultimate goal of the game is to rescue your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:34.140
mate from a relatively titanic bowerbird, a task&nbsp;
that seemed downright impossible at the start.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:39.900
But to succeed in the environment of “Webbed,”&nbsp;
you can’t think like a human. You have to think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.780
like a spider. Once I got used to having a body&nbsp;
light as air, I began to swing about with much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:51.540
greater success. With practice, I even managed to&nbsp;
pull off some rather fancy movement techniques.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:58.560
My fly-catching webs also improved. At first, my&nbsp;
few meager strands only ensnared a meal on rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:04.200
occasions. But the more I crafted wider networks&nbsp;
closer in design to those of real-world spiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:10.320
the more I found my food came to me. One of the&nbsp;
most impressive aspects of “Webbed” is how game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.320 --> 00:04:15.960
mechanics come together with its virtual ecosystem&nbsp;
to make you inhabit a perspective you otherwise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:21.480
might never consider. Granted, “Webbed” is not&nbsp;
trying to perfectly simulate the experience of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:27.240
being a spider. Spiders, generally, can’t fire&nbsp;
off a never-ending barrage of webs like they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.240 --> 00:04:32.220
Spider-Man. They also can’t do sick tricks on a&nbsp;
skateboard, which is something you can eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:37.740
unlock. But the core gameplay truly does excel&nbsp;
at making you feel like part of an ecosystem.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:44.820
Another game that can successfully hijack your&nbsp;
mind is “Gibbon: Beyond the Trees.” In this game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:50.700
you live the fast-paced, high stakes life of a&nbsp;
gibbon swinging full tilt through the rainforest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:57.180
canopy. Like in “Webbed,” your method of traversal&nbsp;
is unintuitive at first, but with practice it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.180 --> 00:05:02.820
starts to feel unbelievably natural, with every&nbsp;
movement a glorious expression of momentum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.820 --> 00:05:07.980
There’s a certain feeling you get when you&nbsp;
perfectly sync up with your fellow ape, releasing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:13.680
at just the right moment for an incredible rush&nbsp;
of speed. It feels astonishingly good to grab a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.080
vine and hold it for a long swing, letting go&nbsp;
at the very top and gaining tremendous height.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.080 --> 00:05:24.660
Though the ecosystem itself is less interactive&nbsp;
than some of the others in this video, it’s still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:29.460
deeply immersive, with other animals finding&nbsp;
their own path through the branches, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.460 --> 00:05:35.160
weather changing at unexpected times. “Gibbon’s”&nbsp;
sense of spontaneity is bolstered by the game’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:41.040
use of procedural animation — or animation that&nbsp;
responds dynamically to an environment, instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.020
of following a predetermined loop. It’s the same&nbsp;
animation method that makes the creatures of Rain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:51.240
World feel so unpredictable. Since I’ve already&nbsp;
covered “Rain World” in detail elsewhere on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:56.520
channel, I won’t say too much about its simulated&nbsp;
ecosystem, but it’s another example of a game that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:01.800
can be so mechanically immersive, it changes your&nbsp;
perspective as your play it: making you think and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:07.320
react like a prey animal living in an environment&nbsp;
that isn’t centered around you. “Gibbon,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:12.960
similarly, has these moments that convey your&nbsp;
character’s utter insignificance. There are places&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:19.320
where the camera zooms all the way out to capture&nbsp;
an incredible leap across an immense chasm. Part&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.320 --> 00:06:23.760
of what makes these moments so breathtaking&nbsp;
is the knowledge that you aren’t watching some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:29.940
predetermined animation — if you make a single&nbsp;
mistake, you will absolutely fall. If you stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.940 --> 00:06:34.980
thinking with your gibbon brain and miss a branch,&nbsp;
you’ll come crashing to the floor below, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:40.500
isn’t such a big deal when you’re close to the&nbsp;
ground, but definitely is in the high canopy. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:45.900
effect is a world that is not only expansive, but&nbsp;
indifferent to the player in a way that makes the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:53.040
environment feel all the vaster. “Planet of Lana”&nbsp;
has its own share of zoom-out-moments that let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.560
you soak in the sheer scale of the world around&nbsp;
you. But unlike in “Gibbon,” it’s not just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:04.320
environments that are massive — but the entities&nbsp;
that inhabit them. Amidst a graveyard of giant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:11.160
fractured skeletons, you can find, in the gloom&nbsp;
of a cave, the many glowing eyes of a monstruous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:18.360
spider-like entity — who towers over you to&nbsp;
such an extent it seems… unfair. Some mechanical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.360 --> 00:07:24.000
entities stand even taller, and while for much&nbsp;
of the game the largest units appear only in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:28.680
background — there are some sequences where you&nbsp;
get up close with these machines that are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:34.260
spellbinding. Rarely have I felt less significant&nbsp;
to a game’s world than I did during these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.260 --> 00:07:40.380
sections. But the most stupefying expression of&nbsp;
scale in “Planet of Lana” doesn’t involve robots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.380 --> 00:07:46.080
or giant monsters. Instead, it’s a relatively&nbsp;
quiet moment where your character runs through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:52.500
an empty expanse with their animal companion. The&nbsp;
camera starts pulling back: revealing the stars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.500 --> 00:07:58.440
overhead. You watch as your character appears to&nbsp;
grow smaller and smaller, and you think surely the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.440 --> 00:08:04.920
perspective can’t get any wider, but the zoom-out&nbsp;
just… keeps going. You become nothing but a tiny&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:10.440
dot in a corner of the screen, and still the&nbsp;
frame isn’t done expanding as the sun starts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:16.680
to peak over the horizon. I timed it, and it takes&nbsp;
almost three real world minutes for the sequence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.680 --> 00:08:22.380
to conclude. But absolutely I could not have told&nbsp;
you this number while playing, because while I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.380 --> 00:08:28.740
watched the sun rise over the distant mountains,&nbsp;
time just sort of… lost all meaning. The rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.740 --> 00:08:34.920
the world faded away, leaving only myself and&nbsp;
the infinite vastness of the cosmos above.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:41.040
Though there are other parts of “Planet of Lana”&nbsp;
that are technically more bombastic and cinematic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.200
it’s this section that made me truly feel&nbsp;
like a small part of something much greater.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:54.780
I’ve gotten a similar feeling of… ‘smallness’&nbsp;
while playing open world games, sometimes due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.780 --> 00:09:00.780
to the density of their worlds, and sometimes due&nbsp;
to the sheer scope. Many modern titles can instill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:06.180
a sense of insignificance simply from how long&nbsp;
it takes to cross their map. But where most open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:11.940
world games differ from the indie titles mentioned&nbsp;
so far is that while the player is typically a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:17.700
minute part of the environment, they can often&nbsp;
change the ecosystem around them to a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:24.000
degree. This can cause players to feel above an&nbsp;
ecosystem rather than part of it — but it can also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.000 --> 00:09:30.120
pull them in deeper. And one open world I found&nbsp;
myself particularly drawn into was Hyrule from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.120 --> 00:09:36.120
“The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild.” The&nbsp;
land of Hyrule isn’t just varied or simply ‘big’&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:42.900
but also a world that requires you to understand&nbsp;
and interact with its systems to progress. As you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.900 --> 00:09:48.300
play, you learn to chop down trees to cross&nbsp;
gaps, and to cook meals. You find ways to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.300 --> 00:09:53.760
overcome the freezing cold of the tundra and the&nbsp;
blistering heat of the desert. A lot of people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:59.580
have already talked about how immersive this world&nbsp;
feels — and I certainly agree. But I think I felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:04.800
even closer to the world when I played “Tears&nbsp;
of the Kingdom.” A direct sequel to “Breath of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:10.020
the Wild,” “Tears of the Kingdom” takes you back&nbsp;
to the same Hyrule, and yet I found myself more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.020 --> 00:10:15.600
immersed in the cell-shaded environments than I&nbsp;
ever was in the game’s predecessor. A big reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:21.120
for this difference is Fuse, a super bizarre new&nbsp;
mechanic that lets you merge practically anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.120 --> 00:10:26.340
in the environment with whatever’s in your hand.&nbsp;
It’s an ability that made me think more about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:31.620
world around me without even realizing it. Because&nbsp;
most objects in the world now had a special&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:37.500
utility that Fuse could bring out, I found myself&nbsp;
engaging more with the virtual ecosystem around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:42.840
me. I explored each section of the world more&nbsp;
thoroughly. I started to know different types of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:48.420
berries by sight alone. I learned more about all&nbsp;
the different elemental interactions the game’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:53.940
psychics system makes possible. The simple act&nbsp;
of experimenting with different in-game-systems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:59.640
became one of my favorite activities. I also found&nbsp;
myself seeking out different enemy types just to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.640 --> 00:11:04.980
discover what sort of Fuse materials I could&nbsp;
obtain. My curiosity was further encouraged by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.260
the greater variety of creatures in “Tears of the&nbsp;
Kingdom,” which exhibit a wider array of emergent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:15.780
behaviors that make the ecosystem feel more&nbsp;
unpredictable. I felt like the game was rewarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:21.240
me for learning how its world functioned,&nbsp;
pushing me to discover all its hidden networks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:28.200
There’s another, blockier open-world game&nbsp;
that rewards you for intimate knowledge of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:34.920
ecosystem. Like Zelda, Minecraft balances huge,&nbsp;
lonely environments with deep systems of learnable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.920 --> 00:11:41.220
interactivity. While much of Minecraft has to do&nbsp;
with… mining, it’s also very much a game about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.220 --> 00:11:46.680
knowing what plants are edible, what types of&nbsp;
animals respond positively to what types of crops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:52.320
what can be turned into your ally and what sees&nbsp;
you as food. It might not immediately strike you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:57.360
as a game that makes you part of its ecosystem,&nbsp;
but personally, when I’m in the early stages of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.360 --> 00:12:02.400
a playthrough and I hear the vocalizations of&nbsp;
giant spiders my brain is still conditioned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:07.440
go on alert like a prey animal. Knowing things&nbsp;
about the world like ‘don’t get too close to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.440 --> 00:12:12.420
weird green guy,’ or ‘don’t look the lanky&nbsp;
fellow directly in the eyes’ are absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.420 --> 00:12:18.660
key to survival. Minecraft also puts emphasis on&nbsp;
different biomes, encouraging the player to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.660 --> 00:12:25.200
what can be found in which type of environment as&nbsp;
they navigate a near-infinite landscape. Tears of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.060
the Kingdom similarly incentivizes knowledge of&nbsp;
different environments — retaining not just the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:35.400
different biomes present in Breath of the Wild,&nbsp;
but adding two all new ecosystems that feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:40.500
radically different from one another. The game is&nbsp;
essentially three worlds stacked on top of each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:40.500 --> 00:12:46.140
other: the bright and sunny islands in the sky,&nbsp;
the vast and varied surface world, and the dark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:52.140
and eerie depths below. Even more than different&nbsp;
Minecraft biomes, all of these ecosystems have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.140 --> 00:12:57.420
their own rules, and their own aesthetics that&nbsp;
reinforce those rules. The depths are meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:02.940
be this hostile, lost realm of foreboding flora&nbsp;
and fauna, which is why I thought it was clever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:08.940
that the plants here seem to take inspiration from&nbsp;
prehistoric species. Even the insects down here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.940 --> 00:13:14.280
look more like something from the Cambrian era.&nbsp;
And what you find in each realm encourages you to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:14.280 --> 00:13:19.860
explore the others. The Sundelion flowers of the&nbsp;
sky islands give you resistance to the depths.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:25.440
Likewise, the Brightbloom Seeds you find in caves&nbsp;
in the sky and on the surface are absolutely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.440 --> 00:13:30.960
crucial for lighting your way when exploring the&nbsp;
dark underground. And when exploring the depths,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:36.420
you’ll find powerful fuse materials that make you&nbsp;
feel more equipped to take on challenges in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.420 --> 00:13:43.260
other two realms. Minecraft has its own parallel&nbsp;
realm in the Nether, a fiery dimension filled with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.260 --> 00:13:49.020
creatures and biomes completely different from&nbsp;
the overworld. As is the case in Zelda, if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:54.120
aren’t careful, the different rules of this domain&nbsp;
can catch you off guard, and lead to disaster.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.200 --> 00:14:00.540
There’re other open world games that succeed&nbsp;
in making you feel like the underdog. “Horizon:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:05.580
Zero Dawn,” with its artificial ecosystem&nbsp;
of towering machines, and Subnautica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.580 --> 00:14:11.580
with its Thalassophobia-inducing ecosystem of&nbsp;
undersea nightmares, both fit this category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:17.040
nicely. As was the case with Rain World, I’ve&nbsp;
already covered these virtual realms in detail,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:22.200
but if you want to feel like a very minute part&nbsp;
of a large food chain — they would be among my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:27.420
top recommendations. It's also worth noting that&nbsp;
as you progress through both Minecraft and Zelda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.420 --> 00:14:33.000
you might start to feel less and less beholden&nbsp;
to the food chain as your abilities grow. Indeed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:33.000 --> 00:14:36.840
there’s the argument to be made that&nbsp;
you’re not part really of these ecosystems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:36.840 --> 00:14:42.660
but conquering them. It’s a feeling that can arise&nbsp;
as you near the end of a lot of open world titles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.660 --> 00:14:48.120
But even in the late stages of Minecraft and&nbsp;
Zelda, there’s no way to fully disconnect from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:53.220
the world around you. No matter your experience&nbsp;
level, you can pretty much always be caught off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.220 --> 00:14:58.140
guard by something in the environment. As games&nbsp;
with a significant amount of player freedom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.140 --> 00:15:04.080
how much you disrupt the digital ecosystems of&nbsp;
Minecraft and Zelda is ultimately up to you. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:08.760
the end, the option to keep the land around&nbsp;
you green and unspoiled is always available.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:16.560
Life in an undamaged ecosystem is not an option&nbsp;
in the game “Endling.” “Endling” didn’t begin the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.560 --> 00:15:22.140
way I expected. Based on screenshots, I thought&nbsp;
it was a relatively innocent game about a family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.140 --> 00:15:28.380
of foxes. But when I booted it up, I was greeted&nbsp;
by these two dark shapes floating against a red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:28.380 --> 00:15:34.680
background. The camera pulled out and I realized&nbsp;
those were embryos in a mother fox’s womb, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:40.860
the world outside was on fire. Almost immediately,&nbsp;
I found myself inhabiting the perspective of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:46.980
mother fox I controlled, reduced to the single&nbsp;
instinct of moving forwards. A burning deer sent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:54.300
me hurtling off a ledge. Still, I pressed onwards,&nbsp;
limping, through the smoke and the rain. At last,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:59.580
I made it to shelter, and as a scene played of&nbsp;
the mother having her cubs — I took what felt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:59.580 --> 00:16:04.800
like my first breath since pressing start. But&nbsp;
the scene wasn’t over, because then the camera&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.800 --> 00:16:10.080
panned up out of the den to remind me just what&nbsp;
kind of a world these cubs had been born into…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:16.740
“Endling” is a game that traps you within its&nbsp;
setting. Ingeniously, from here, the gameplay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:16.740 --> 00:16:22.440
changes into a stressful cycle of finding food for&nbsp;
your cubs before the night ends. To reflect this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:28.200
new format, the raging inferno is replaced with&nbsp;
desolate snowfall. Tracking down food in this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:28.200 --> 00:16:33.780
barren ecosystem is a constant challenge, and&nbsp;
to make things more challenging, before long,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:33.780 --> 00:16:39.240
your cubs start coming with you to learn how to&nbsp;
hunt. Trying to remain alive while also protecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.240 --> 00:16:44.700
your pups requires constant vigilance, pulling&nbsp;
you further into the world. Though technically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:44.700 --> 00:16:50.280
post-apocalyptic, “Endling” depicts a future&nbsp;
much closer in time than most works of sci-fi,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.280 --> 00:16:55.740
one ravaged by the mistakes of humanity. As&nbsp;
a game rooted in the perspective of a fox,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:55.740 --> 00:17:00.780
no dialogue communicates its themes&nbsp;
of environmental degradation. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:06.360
the devastation is felt in the bareness of the&nbsp;
ecosystem itself and the decaying remnants that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.360 --> 00:17:12.000
make up the landscape. Humans themselves are&nbsp;
rare, their gas masks making them feel alien,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:12.000 --> 00:17:16.860
and helping you think of them as threats the&nbsp;
same way the fox does. To drag you further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.860 --> 00:17:22.500
into the psyche of an animal, one of your cubs is&nbsp;
carried off early in the game by a human survivor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:22.500 --> 00:17:28.200
But though the game teaches you that the world of&nbsp;
humanity is best avoided, as food becomes scarce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.200 --> 00:17:34.320
you find yourself taking greater and greater risks&nbsp;
just to keep your cubs alive. This all came to a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.320 --> 00:17:39.660
head for me when one night, starving from lack&nbsp;
of things to hunt, I allowed my cubs to feed on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:45.120
noxious-looking garbage, not knowing if it was&nbsp;
toxic. It’s a moment that wouldn’t be nearly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:50.760
powerful if I hadn’t felt forced to make that&nbsp;
choice myself due to the ecosystem’s scarcity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:58.680
The later stages of “Gibbon” uses in-game systems&nbsp;
to convey ecological devastation in an analogous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.680 --> 00:18:04.440
manner. After a while, you start seeing plumes&nbsp;
of smoke and clear-cut groves in the background.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:10.020
Swinging itself begins to feel different&nbsp;
as the number of trees decline. Eventually,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:16.440
a full-scale fire breaks out, sending the forest&nbsp;
ecosystem into a panic. Missing a branch holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:21.660
new weight, as falling into the forest below&nbsp;
now means succumbing to the inferno. Crossing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:26.820
large gaps are now key for survival — turning&nbsp;
what was once a joyous expression of freedom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.820 --> 00:18:33.240
into a grim necessity. Even later, the natural&nbsp;
forests give way to rows of monoculture crops&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.240 --> 00:18:39.180
that are difficult to swing through, which then&nbsp;
transition into urban areas. Here again, “Gibbon”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:44.340
doesn’t need dialogue to convey its themes.&nbsp;
The simple mechanical awkwardness of trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:44.340 --> 00:18:49.380
navigate rooftops instead of the branches you’re&nbsp;
accustomed to says more than a direct statement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.380 --> 00:18:55.500
on environmental destruction potentially could&nbsp;
have. Maybe it should be no surprise that games&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:01.140
that make you part of an ecosystem can get you to&nbsp;
think more about the world around you. After all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.140 --> 00:19:06.720
humans are already part of an ecosystem, even&nbsp;
if it doesn’t always feel that way in our daily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:06.720 --> 00:19:12.000
lives. Though we are a relatively insignificant&nbsp;
part of Earth’s history on a cosmic scale,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.000 --> 00:19:17.160
we have the capacity to profoundly influence our&nbsp;
environment in the present. And after playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:17.160 --> 00:19:21.720
through some of these games, I can’t help but&nbsp;
wonder what kind of ending we’re headed towards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:27.900
If there’s an ecosystem-focused game with a&nbsp;
more hopeful — if bittersweet — conclusion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:27.900 --> 00:19:33.840
I’d say it’s probably “Planet of Lana.” It’s&nbsp;
a game that exposes you to constant imagery of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:39.660
the mechanical interrupting the natural world, and&nbsp;
yet it’s also very much a story about how you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:39.660 --> 00:19:45.000
learn from the mistakes of the past to create a&nbsp;
better tomorrow. As you progress through the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.000 --> 00:19:50.280
educating yourself on the ins and outs of the&nbsp;
environment, you gain a tremendous appreciation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:50.280 --> 00:19:55.380
for the world around you, all without a single&nbsp;
sentence of dialogue spoken. And while I won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.380 --> 00:20:00.960
spoil the final moments, I will say the ending&nbsp;
stayed with me long after the credits rolled.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:08.220
Ultimately, there’s no single way to create&nbsp;
an effective ecosystem-based game. Across many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.220 --> 00:20:12.720
different genres and art-styles, game&nbsp;
studios have made a startling variety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:18.000
of worlds ruled by interactive systems. And&nbsp;
there’s plenty of games with unique fictional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:18.000 --> 00:20:23.460
ecosystems that I wasn’t able to cover here.&nbsp;
If you’re interested in virtual ecosystems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:30.060
I’ve got a full list of the games featured in&nbsp;
this video down below. And as always, thanks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:35.700
for watching. If you enjoyed this entry, please&nbsp;
lend your support by liking, subscribing, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:41.220
hitting the notification icon to stay up to date&nbsp;
on all things Curious. See you in the next video.

